{"prompt":"Hi, I need a microfluidic chip for a leukapheresis-derived white blood cell enrichment assay with droplet-based QC of the recovered leukocyte fractions. Please treat inlet_1 as the diluted leukapheresis product, inlet_2 as PBS with 2% FBS and 2 mM EDTA wash buffer, and inlet_3 as a fluorescent anti-CD45/viability-stain reagent stream. Please design it so that:

• the leukapheresis sample from inlet_1 can go directly to outlet_5 as a bulk discard or overflow route;
• the same leukapheresis sample from inlet_1 can also go directly to outlet_2 for bulk fraction collection;
• the staining reagent from inlet_3 passes through tesla_valve_1, then through chamber_2, and then to outlet_3, with chamber_2 sized at 5292 µm in length and 5998 µm in width;
• the wash buffer from inlet_2 can go directly to outlet_3;
• the inlet_3 reagent line can also be purged through tesla_valve_1 directly to outlet_5;
• the leukapheresis sample from inlet_1 can also be diverted directly to outlet_4;
• the inlet_3 reagent stream should also be able to pass through tesla_valve_1, then chamber_2, then chamber_3, and then feed droplet_2 for encapsulation, with chamber_3 sized at 4205 µm in length and 5564 µm in width, and droplet_2 using a 186 µm nozzle width;
• the wash buffer from inlet_2 can also be routed directly to outlet_5;
• the leukapheresis sample from inlet_1 should be able to enter chamber_1 and then feed droplet_1 for an initial leukocyte QC emulsion, with droplet_1 using a 176 µm nozzle width;
• droplets made from the fluid exiting chamber_1 should be guided to outlet_1;
• material in chamber_2 should also be able to drain from chamber_2 to outlet_3;
• droplets from droplet_1 should go to outlet_1;
• material from chamber_3 should feed droplet_2;
• droplets made from the fluid exiting chamber_3 should be guided through chamber_4 to outlet_4;
• droplets made from the fluid exiting chamber_3 should also be guided through chamber_4 to droplet_3;
• droplets from droplet_2 should be able to pass through chamber_4 and then go to outlet_4;
• droplets from droplet_2 should also be able to pass through chamber_4 and then feed droplet_3;
• droplets made from the fluid exiting chamber_4 should be guided to outlet_5;
• droplets from droplet_3 should go to outlet_5, and droplet_3 should have a 33 µm nozzle width."}

{"prompt":"Hello, I’m looking for a microfluidic chip design for an aptamer selection assay: specifically, an early-round protein-binding SELEX workflow to screen a randomized ssDNA library against human thrombin.

- Please route the randomized ssDNA library in SELEX binding buffer from the first input port through the first delaying curved channel, then continue that same stream through the second delaying curved channel, and finally send it to the fluid outlet. In the context of the SELEX assay, I want the first delay region to give the oligonucleotide pool time to refold properly before target exposure, and then have that library stream remain in the downstream delay path before collection at the outlet.

- Also route the thrombin solution, introduced from the second input port, directly through the second delaying curved channel and then to the same fluid outlet. For the assay, this second inlet is the target stream that meets the preconditioned ssDNA library in that downstream delay region, so the second delaying curved channel serves as the binding-incubation segment before the reacted SELEX mixture is collected at the outlet."}

{"prompt":"Hi, could you design a microfluidic chip for an acetaminophen-induced liver-on-chip hepatotoxicity assay using primary human hepatocyte spheroids, Williams’ E maintenance medium, acetaminophen dosing medium, and a downstream readout of injury-associated extracellular vesicles versus larger necrotic debris? I’d like inlet_2 to load the hepatocyte spheroids directly into chamber_2, and I also want inlet_2 to be able to perfuse maintenance medium through chamber_2 and send the effluent to outlet_6. Chamber_2 is the main hepatocyte culture region and should be 970 µm long and 3421 µm wide. For the dosing branch, acetaminophen-containing medium entering at inlet_4 should pass through filter_2 and then into mixer_1, and that same inlet_4 stream should also be routable through filter_2 and mixer_1 to outlet_2 for an upstream collected sample. The filtered dosing stream leaving filter_2 should enter mixer_1, and filter_2 itself should be 14173 µm long and 4529 µm wide, with hexagonal posts of 408 µm diameter arranged in 4 columns and 3 rows. I want the output of mixer_1 to be able to go directly to outlet_3, and I also need the mixed drug stream from mixer_1 to perfuse chamber_2 and then drain to outlet_6. In addition, the inlet_4 dosing stream should be able to continue through filter_2, mixer_1, and chamber_2 to outlet_6 for the actual hepatotoxic exposure run.

Please include a separate media-conditioning line where inlet_1 feeds mixer_2, with inlet_1 also able to drive flow through mixer_2 to outlet_6; the outlet of mixer_2 itself should discharge to outlet_6. Mixer_2 should be a looped mixer. For particle handling, I want a mixed injury sample introduced at inlet_3 to split by particle size so that the smaller particles go through tesla_valve_1 and are collected at outlet_4, while the larger particles from inlet_3 go to outlet_6. Tesla_valve_1 should then empty to outlet_4, and it should have 10 segment pairs with a segment length of 2291 µm and a segment width of 799 µm. I also need filter_1 to be a size-separation filter; from filter_1, the smaller particles should go into tesla_valve_1, those smaller particles should then pass through tesla_valve_1 to outlet_4, and the larger particles from filter_1 should be removed to outlet_1.

For a secondary downstream sampling branch, I want the filtered dosing mixture from filter_2 to pass through mixer_1, then through chamber_1, then through tesla_valve_2, and be collected at outlet_5. Chamber_1 should also feed directly into tesla_valve_2. From chamber_1, the stream should be able to continue through tesla_valve_2 and then through tesla_valve_3 into tesla_valve_4. Downstream of that, flow leaving tesla_valve_3 should pass through tesla_valve_4 and then go to outlet_6, and tesla_valve_4 itself should also discharge to outlet_6. Tesla_valve_2 should have 9 segment pairs with a segment length of 1825 µm and a segment width of 673 µm, and tesla_valve_3 should have 9 segment pairs with a segment length of 1855 µm and a segment width of 1803 µm."}

{"prompt":"Hi, I need a chip design for a C2C12 myotube glucose-uptake and lactate-release monitoring assay.

Q: Can you set the layout for this assay?
A: Please send the defined glucose/lactate assay medium from inlet_2 through mixer_1 and then to outlet_2, and make mixer_1 a toroidal mixer. Please keep a direct bypass from inlet_1 with the C2C12 culture medium to outlet_9. Please keep another direct line from inlet_1 to outlet_3. Please route the assay medium from inlet_2 to outlet_7. Please route the assay medium from inlet_2 into chamber_1 for the cell exposure step. Please route the assay medium from inlet_2 through tesla_valve_1 to outlet_10. Please also load the C2C12 cell suspension from inlet_1 into chamber_1. Please keep direct assay-medium lines from inlet_2 to outlet_1, outlet_5, outlet_9, outlet_4, and outlet_6. Please let the flow from inlet_1 pass through chamber_1 and then go to outlet_8 for metabolite collection. Chamber_1 itself should also drain to outlet_8. The output of tesla_valve_1 should go to outlet_10, and tesla_valve_1 should have 5 segment pairs, with segment length 1476 µm and segment width 1432 µm."}

{"prompt":"Design a microfluidic chip for synthesis of siRNA-loaded lipid nanoparticles for siRNA delivery. I want to use one premixed nanoparticle formulation stream, with siRNA, ionizable lipid, helper lipids, cholesterol, PEG-lipid, and the solvent/buffer mixture, and let the chip control the timed self-assembly and maturation of the particles.

The formulation should enter from inlet_1 and go first through delay_1, then through delay_2, and then to delay_3, so the stream gets a defined residence time before the final particle-formation stage. After that, the material coming from delay_1 should continue through delay_2, then delay_3, then into mixer_1, then through chamber_1, and finally leave from outlet_1, so the siRNA-loaded lipid nanoparticles can finish mixing and stabilize before collection. Also, the stream that reaches delay_3 should be routed from delay_3 through mixer_1, then through chamber_1, and then to outlet_1 for the final nanoparticle recovery.

For the geometry, chamber_1 should have a length of 4838 µm and a width of 2820 µm. Delay_2 should be a serpentine delay with 15 turnings, an amplitude of 5194 µm, and 77 µm between turnings. Delay_3 should be another serpentine delay with 12 turnings, an amplitude of 6269 µm, and 132 µm between turnings. Mixer_1 should be a circular mixer."}

{"prompt":"Hi, I need a microfluidic chip for an amperometric salivary lactate sensing assay with on-chip sample conditioning and calibration. Please design it so that:

• the PBS/KCl blank or rinse from inlet_2 can go directly to outlet_2;
• the ferri/ferrocyanide calibration solution from inlet_1 runs through mixer_3 and mixer_4 to outlet_1;
• the inlet_2 stream can also be routed through mixer_4 to outlet_1 to precondition the downstream sensing line;
• the salivary lactate sample from inlet_2 feeds mixer_1, and mixer_1 should be a twisted mixer with 9 turnings, a 2153 µm amplitude, and 138 µm between turnings;
• after mixer_1, the conditioned sample can pass through delay_1 to outlet_2 for timed collection;
• mixer_1 should also be able to discharge directly to outlet_1 for a rapid baseline readout;
• for the main assay route, the conditioned sample from mixer_1 should go through delay_1, then mixer_3 and mixer_4, to outlet_1;
• the delayed sample from delay_1 should also be able to enter mixer_2 for secondary conditioning;
• from delay_1, the sample should continue through mixer_2 and mixer_3 into mixer_4;
• for the full downstream measurement path, route the delay_1 stream through mixer_2, mixer_3, and mixer_4 to outlet_1;
• in a shorter downstream mode, route the delay_1 stream through mixer_3 and mixer_4 to outlet_1;
• the outlet from mixer_2 should likewise pass through mixer_3 and mixer_4 to outlet_1;
• and mixer_4 itself should empty to outlet_1."}

{"prompt":"Hi, I’d like a microfluidic chip for a droplet RT-LAMP malaria assay, specifically for detecting Plasmodium falciparum nucleic acid from lysed finger-prick blood. Please route the RT-LAMP primer/enzyme mastermix from fluid entry 4 through mixing microchannel 2 and then into tesla valve 3; that mixing microchannel 2 should be a looped mixer. In parallel, send the cofactor/buffer stream from fluid entry 5 through mixing microchannel 1 to tesla valve 3. The patient lysate carrying the parasite target should also go from fluid entry 3 directly to tesla valve 3, and that same sample stream should continue from fluid entry 3 through tesla valve 3 into microdroplet generator 2. I also need the internal amplification control introduced from fluid entry 1 straight to tesla valve 3.

For the reporter branch, form droplets from the fluorogenic probe mix entering at fluid entry 2 and guide those droplets to tesla valve 1. Then carry that droplet stream made from fluid entry 2 through tesla valve 1 and tesla valve 4 into microdroplet generator 2, with tesla valve 4 itself also feeding into microdroplet generator 2. The conditioned reagent stream leaving mixing microchannel 2 should likewise pass through tesla valve 3 and then into microdroplet generator 2. Microdroplet generator 2 needs to be a hydrodynamic-focusing droplet generator.

I also want the auxiliary droplet line preserved: droplets produced at microdroplet generator 1 should go through tesla valve 1 to exit point 1, and another route from microdroplet generator 1 should go through tesla valve 1 to tesla valve 2. From tesla valve 2, continue on to tesla valve 3 so that auxiliary control droplets can be brought back into the main malaria assay path. Finally, any droplets formed from the material exiting tesla valve 3 should be guided to exit point 2 for downstream readout."}

{"prompt":"Hi, I’d like a microfluidic chip for a single-nucleus RNA-seq NGS library preparation workflow with droplet barcoding from a crude tissue homogenate. Load the crude nuclei suspension in nuclei isolation buffer through inlet_1 into filter_1. The smaller debris and free-RNA fraction from inlet_1 should go to outlet_1. The larger-particle fraction from inlet_1 should be sent onward to filter_2. I want the largest aggregates from that large-particle branch of filter_1 sent through chamber_1 and then to outlet_2, while the smaller subfraction of that same large-particle branch goes through tesla_valve_2, then delay_2, and exits at outlet_3. The smaller-particle fraction coming out of filter_2 should also pass through tesla_valve_2, through delay_2, and leave via outlet_3. Chamber_1 should additionally have a path into tesla_valve_1, and material from chamber_1 should also be able to go through delay_2 to outlet_3.

For the barcoding chemistry, feed the reverse-transcription and indexing master mix from inlet_4 to tesla_valve_1. I also need that same inlet_4 line to have a direct route to outlet_2, and a separate route from inlet_4 through delay_2 to outlet_3. From inlet_4, carry the reagent through tesla_valve_1 to delay_1, and then from inlet_4 through tesla_valve_1 and delay_1 into droplet_1. Please make droplet_1 a flow-focusing droplet generator. The droplets formed from the fluid exiting delay_1 should be guided through chamber_2 into delay_2, and those same droplets should continue through delay_2 to outlet_3. Droplets leaving droplet_1 should enter chamber_2, and droplets from droplet_1 should also be able to pass through chamber_2, then delay_2, and exit at outlet_3. Chamber_2 itself should drain through delay_2 to outlet_3.

For downstream stabilization and wash handling around the droplet barcoding stage, send the cDNA stabilization buffer from inlet_2 through delay_2 to outlet_3, and also allow inlet_2 to pass through chamber_2 and then through delay_2 to outlet_3. The stop buffer from inlet_3 should go directly to outlet_3."}

{"prompt":"Hi, I need a microfluidic chip for a multiplexed bead-based cytokine profiling assay on stimulated whole-blood material, recombinant cytokine standards, and a QC serum, using color-coded antibody capture beads and a biotinylated detection-antibody cocktail. Please route the stimulated whole-blood suspension from inlet 6 into reaction unit 2, and also allow the stream from inlet 6 to continue through reaction unit 2 to microfilter 1. The effluent of reaction unit 2 should feed microfilter 1, but I also need a direct route for the entire unsplit suspension leaving reaction unit 2, with both large and small particulates still together, to fluid outlet 1. Microfilter 1 should be a DLD section 14024 µm long and 4739 µm wide, with triangular posts of 13 µm, a row-shift fraction of 0.21, and a critical particle diameter of 20.14 µm, and both the larger-particle and smaller-particle fractions from that filter should go to fluid outlet.

Use inlet 5 for the barcoded capture-bead master mix and send it into reaction unit 1, which should be 4799 µm long and 4085 µm wide; that same bead stream should also be able to pass through reaction unit 1 to the microdroplet generator. I also need droplets formed from the fluid exiting reaction unit 1 to go to fluid outlet. For the detection-antibody cocktail at inlet 2, keep a direct line to fluid outlet, a line through microfilter 2 to fluid outlet, and a path from inlet 2 through delay channel 1 to the microdroplet generator. The delay channel should be a 14-turn serpentine with an amplitude of 5505 µm and 109 µm between turns, and droplets formed from the fluid exiting that delay channel should go to fluid outlet 1. Microfilter 2 should be a pillar-array filter, 7609 µm long and 4651 µm wide, with 662 µm posts arranged in 6 columns and 2 rows, and the outlet of microfilter 2 should also be routable to fluid outlet 1. The microdroplet generator should have a nozzle width of 101 µm.

For the analytical droplet runs, let inlet 1 carry patient plasma premixed off-chip with the coded bead panel, inlet 3 carry recombinant cytokine standards premixed the same way, and inlet 7 carry the QC control serum with the same bead mix. I need droplets made from the fluid entering inlet 3 to go directly to fluid outlet, and I also need droplets made from inlet 3 to enter reaction unit 3. Droplets made from inlet 1 should be able to go directly to fluid outlet 1, to enter reaction unit 3, and to pass through reaction unit 3 to microfilter 3. Droplets made from inlet 7 should be able to go directly to fluid outlet 1 and also pass through reaction unit 3 to microfilter 3. The output of reaction unit 3 should feed microfilter 3, and reaction unit 3 should also connect through microfilter 3 to fluid outlet 1; the outlet of microfilter 3 itself should go to fluid outlet 1. Make microfilter 3 a pillar-array filter 5053 µm long and 3943 µm wide with square posts of 739 µm in a 7-column by 7-row layout. Finally, use inlet 4 as a direct wash-buffer line to fluid outlet 1."}

{"prompt":"Hi, can you design a microfluidic chip for a droplet-based sandwich ELISA to quantify IL-6 in serum? I want inlet_1 to carry a preincubated assay mix of patient serum, anti-IL-6 capture microbeads, HRP-labeled detection antibody, and assay buffer, and inlet_2 to carry the fluorogenic HRP substrate.

Q: What flow sequence do I need on the chip?
A: Send the substrate from inlet_2 through delay_1 and then through tesla_valve_2 into droplet_1 so the substrate reaches the droplet-generation stage after a controlled delay. Feed the IL-6 immunocomplex suspension from inlet_1 through mixer_1 and also allow that same stream to go to outlet_2. From inlet_1 after it passes through mixer_1, route one branch to tesla_valve_3 for a parallel assay branch, and route another branch to tesla_valve_2 so it can meet the delayed substrate stream. From mixer_1, send another branch through tesla_valve_1 to outlet_1. Also keep the direct mixer_1 connection to tesla_valve_3 for that immunoassay branch. Route droplets formed from the tesla_valve_3 effluent through tesla_valve_4 and into filter_1. Route droplets formed from the tesla_valve_2 effluent to tesla_valve_4 as well. After tesla_valve_4, pass the processed ELISA droplets through filter_1, then through tesla_valve_5 and mixer_2 to outlet_2. Also keep the tesla_valve_5 stream continuing through mixer_2 to outlet_2. Use a sieving filter for filter_1, and make mixer_1 a circular mixer."}

{"prompt":"Hi, I need a microfluidic chip for a continuous siRNA-loaded PBAE polyplex synthesis and size-fractionation assay. Feed a crude, pre-mixed suspension of siRNA/PBAE nanocomplexes in citrate buffer, containing well-formed polyplex nanoparticles, free siRNA, and larger aggregates, from the entry point into the first delay microchannel. Then carry that same synthesis stream from the entry point through the first delay microchannel to the first microfilter, and from the delay microchannel through the first microfilter to the second microfilter. The first delay microchannel should be a 20-turn serpentine with an amplitude of 3792 µm and 203 µm between turnings. The first microfilter should be 8359 µm long and 1154 µm wide, with 790 µm posts arranged in 5 columns and 7 rows. Use the second microfilter as a DLD stage, and use the third microfilter as a DLD stage as well.

From the second DLD microfilter, route the smaller, on-target siRNA polyplex particles through the sixth microfilter to the tesla valve; continue that smaller-particle stream from the second microfilter through the sixth microfilter, through the tesla valve, through the mixer, and into the seventh microfilter. In parallel, send the smaller particles from the second microfilter through the sixth microfilter and through the first tesla valve to the second outlet. Send the larger-particle fraction from the second microfilter to the fifth microfilter. From the third DLD microfilter, direct the larger aggregates through the fourth microfilter to the second outlet, and direct the smaller particles through the fifth microfilter to the sixth microfilter. The fourth microfilter should be a pillar-matrix filter, 4385 µm long and 5797 µm wide, with 461 µm posts in 4 columns and 5 rows; it should also have a direct path from the fourth microfilter to the second outlet. The fifth microfilter should be 7389 µm long and 5410 µm wide, with hexagonal 399 µm posts in 10 columns and 2 rows.

After the fifth microfilter, route material through the sixth microfilter and the tesla valve to the first mixer, and also allow the path from the fifth microfilter through the sixth microfilter and the tesla valve to discharge to the second outlet. Route flow from the fourth microfilter through the mixer to the seventh microfilter. Also send flow from the sixth microfilter through the tesla valve to the first mixer, and from the sixth microfilter through the first tesla valve to the second outlet. The first tesla valve itself should connect directly to the second outlet, and it should also feed the mixer so that flow from the first tesla valve can go through the mixer to the seventh microfilter. Finally, send flow from the mixer through the seventh microfilter to the first outlet, and keep a direct path from the seventh microfilter to the first outlet. The seventh microfilter should be 3500 µm long and 4749 µm wide, with diamond-shaped posts of 129 µm and 5 rows."}

{"prompt":"1. I need a microfluidic chip for a sepsis biomarker panel assay, using patient plasma that is already premixed off-chip with a fluorescent antibody cocktail for markers like IL-6, procalcitonin, TNF-alpha, and IL-8. The sample should enter from inlet_1 and first go through delay_1 to delay_2, so there is an incubation stage before the readout part. Delay_1 should be a serpentine delay with 16 turnings, an amplitude of 2651 µm, and 253 µm between turnings.

2. After that, the same plasma-antibody mixture should continue from inlet_1 through delay_1, then through delay_2, then into mixer_1, and finally go to outlet_1, so the biomarker binding mixture can be well conditioned before collection. Mixer_1 should be a looped mixer with a diameter of 1516 µm, 5 circles, and 405 µm distance between circles.

3. Also, the reacted mixture coming out of mixer_1 should go to outlet_1 for collection as the final sepsis panel sample."}

{"prompt":"I need a microfluidic chip for a gut-on-chip perfusion monitoring assay, where the recirculating culture medium contains larger detached epithelial cell clusters and smaller extracellular-vesicle-rich particles. Please send the sampled perfusate from inlet_1 into filter_1 first. The larger cell-cluster fraction from inlet_1 should be routed to delay_1, while the smaller particle fraction coming out of filter_1 should go to droplet_1. I also want droplets made from that smaller-particle fraction exiting filter_1 to be sent into chamber_1 so the vesicle-rich sample can be exposed in the organ chamber. 

For the delayed large-particle branch, the flow should continue from delay_1 through delay_2 and delay_3 to delay_4. That same branch should also run from delay_1 through delay_2, delay_3, and delay_4 to tesla_valve_1, and then continue from delay_1 through delay_2, delay_3, delay_4, and tesla_valve_1 to droplet_1 so the delayed cell-cluster perfusate reaches the first droplet stage. Inside that delay train, delay_2 should feed delay_3, delay_2 should also continue through delay_3 to delay_4, and delay_3 should continue through delay_4 to tesla_valve_1. After tesla_valve_1, please form droplets from the exiting perfusate and guide those droplets through chamber_1 to droplet_2. The droplets coming from droplet_1 should also go into chamber_1. Then droplets made from fluid exiting chamber_1 should go to outlet_1, and the output from droplet_2 should also go to outlet_1.

For the component details, chamber_1 should be 4382 µm long and 2148 µm wide. Delay_3 should be a 10-turn delay with an amplitude of 3213 µm and 201 µm between turnings. Delay_4 should also have 10 turnings, with an amplitude of 6371 µm and 390 µm between turnings. Droplet_1 should be a T-junction droplet generator with a nozzle width of 170 µm. Droplet_2 should be a hydrodynamic-focusing droplet generator. Tesla_valve_1 should have 1 segment pair, with segment length 1050 µm and segment width 2228 µm."}

{"prompt":"Design a microfluidic chip for an siRNA delivery particle synthesis assay in which I formulate siRNA-loaded lipid nanoparticles by controlled nanoprecipitation of an MC3/DSPC/cholesterol/DMG-PEG2000 lipid phase in ethanol against siRNA in citrate buffer, with separate conditioning and chase streams. I want the RNase-free PBS chase coming in through the fourth input port to go directly to the first output for final particle collection and dilution. The conditioning buffer introduced at the third input port should be able to pass through the second delaying serpentine microchannel, then through the second Tesla valve, then through the third Tesla valve, and leave through the outlet labeled output. The lipid-in-ethanol formulation entering at the first input port should have a direct path to that same output. The siRNA solution from the second input port should feed into the first delaying serpentine microchannel.

Further downstream, the stream coming out of the second delaying serpentine microchannel should enter the second Tesla valve, and that same conditioned stream should also continue from the second delaying serpentine microchannel through the second Tesla valve to the third Tesla valve. On the nanoparticle assembly side, material leaving the first delaying serpentine microchannel should pass through the first Tesla valve, then through the first reaction chamber, and then go to the first output. Material exiting the first Tesla valve should also be able to pass through the reaction chamber and then reach the first output. I also need the outlet from the third Tesla valve routed to the first output, and the contents of the first reaction chamber routed to the first output.

For the device geometry, the first delaying serpentine microchannel should have 3 turnings, an amplitude of 2171 µm, and 92 µm between turnings. The second delaying serpentine microchannel should have 10 turnings, an amplitude of 6286 µm, and 454 µm between turnings. The first Tesla valve should have 6 segment pairs, with a segment length of 1768 µm and a segment width of 1024 µm. The third Tesla valve should have 8 segment pairs, with a segment length of 2551 µm and a segment width of 2070 µm."}

{"prompt":"Hi, could you help me design a microfluidic chip for a droplet-assisted hybridoma screening assay to identify clones secreting anti-HER2 IgG? I plan to use a hybridoma cell suspension in culture medium from inlet_1, screening buffer from inlet_2, and a fluorescent HER2 antigen probe from inlet_3.

Q: What flow layout should this chip have for that assay?
A: Please form droplets from the fluorescent HER2 probe entering inlet_3 and guide those droplets to outlet_4 for the droplet calibration branch. Let the screening buffer from inlet_2 run directly to outlet_4 for priming and washout. Route the fluorescent HER2 probe from inlet_3 through mixer_4 and then to outlet_5 for the final labeling line. Send the hybridoma suspension from inlet_1 through chamber_1 and then to outlet_4 so the cells can be observed briefly before being cleared. Also allow the hybridoma suspension from inlet_1 to pass through mixer_4 and then to outlet_5. Keep a direct line from inlet_3 to outlet_4 as well for probe flushing. Route the screening buffer from inlet_2 through delay_1 and then through mixer_3 to outlet_3. Route the hybridoma suspension from inlet_1 through mixer_3 to outlet_3. Feed the hybridoma suspension from inlet_1 into mixer_1. Include a direct bypass from inlet_1 to outlet_1. For the main antibody-screening path, send the hybridoma suspension from inlet_1 through mixer_1, through mixer_2, through chamber_2, through tesla_valve_1, through mixer_4, and finally to outlet_5. Also send the screening buffer from inlet_2 through delay_1 and mixer_3 to outlet_2. Keep a direct route from inlet_3 to outlet_3 for the fluorescent HER2 probe. Continue the processed stream from mixer_1 through mixer_2, chamber_2, tesla_valve_1, and mixer_4 to outlet_5. Continue the delayed buffer stream from delay_1 through mixer_3 to outlet_2. Route the output of mixer_2 into chamber_2. Route the output of mixer_3 to outlet_2. Route the output of tesla_valve_1 through mixer_4 to outlet_5. For the component choices, mixer_1 should be toroidal, mixer_2 should be ring-shaped, mixer_3 should be circular, and mixer_4 should be ring-shaped."}

{"prompt":"Hello, I need a microfluidic chip for a droplet-based enzymatic glucose and lactate monitoring assay.

- From the second fluid entry, please form droplets of the lactate assay stream and send those droplets to the first fluid outlet. I want this done with the second microdroplet generator, and that generator should be the hydrodynamic-focusing type.

- From the first fluid entry, please also form droplets of the glucose assay stream and route those droplets to the same first fluid outlet. For this part, use the first microdroplet generator, and its nozzle width should be 28 µm."}

{"prompt":"Hi, could you please help me design a microfluidic chip for a wound-healing / cell migration assay? I want to generate clean, uniform keratinocyte-containing hydrogel droplets for a downstream migration screen, and I would like the layout to follow this workflow:

- Please take the keratinocyte suspension in migration medium from the fluid entry, pass it through particle filter A first, and then send it into the droplet generator. For this assay, particle filter A should be a sieving filter so it can remove cell clumps and debris before droplet formation.

- At the droplet-generation stage, I need the filtered keratinocyte suspension to be formed into droplets using a flow-focusing droplet generator with a 163 µm nozzle width. After the droplets are produced, route them through tesla valve A and then on to the fluid outlet so they can be collected for the migration assay."}

{"prompt":"Hi, please design a microfluidic chip for a droplet-based single-cell transfection workflow, using HEK293 cells in electroporation buffer with a GFP plasmid mix.

1. Take the HEK293/plasmid suspension entering at the fluid inlet, form it into droplets, and send those droplets through the first Tesla valve and on to the second Tesla valve so the transfection emulsion is conditioned along that path.
2. Also route the droplets produced by the first microdroplet generator through the first Tesla valve, then through the second Tesla valve, and finally to the fluid outlet for collection of the transfection droplets. The first microdroplet generator should be a hydrodynamic-focusing droplet generator."}

{"prompt":"Hi, could you help me design a microfluidic chip for a droplet-based wound-healing / cell migration assay, where I package keratinocyte-conditioned migration medium into droplets and give it a defined on-chip delay before collection?

- Please form droplets from the keratinocyte-conditioned medium entering the first input port, then send those droplets through the tesla valve and onward through the first delaying twisted channel and then the second delaying twisted channel, so the wound-healing stimulus has a controlled residence time on chip.
- After that, the droplets coming out of the second delaying twisted channel should go directly to the fluid outlet for collection."}

{"prompt":"Hi, could you help me design a microfluidic chip for a droplet-based sepsis biomarker panel, using diluted EDTA whole blood from suspected sepsis patients as the sample stream and a multiplex antibody/capture reagent mix as the assay reagent stream?

Q: What should the chip do for this sepsis assay?
A: I need the reagent stream entering inlet_2 to be formed directly into droplets and routed to outlet_1 as one droplet population for the panel. From the patient sample entering inlet_1, direct the larger-particle fraction into delay_1 so the cell-rich material is carried into a timing section before encapsulation; delay_1 should be a 6-turn serpentine with an amplitude of 1778 µm and 499 µm between turnings. From filter_1, take the larger particles within the smaller-particle fraction and move that selected stream through chamber_1 and then chamber_2 before it enters droplet_1 for droplet formation. Droplet_1 should have a nozzle width of 73 µm. From filter_2, the smaller-particle fraction should be made into droplets and sent to outlet_1, and filter_2 should be a deterministic lateral displacement stage. The fluid exiting delay_1 should also be converted into droplets and guided to outlet_1. Finally, the material exiting chamber_2 should be formed into droplets and routed to outlet_1."}

{"prompt":"Hi, I need a microfluidic chip for a catalyst reaction screening assay using a suspension of supported catalyst microparticles in substrate solution.

- Please have the catalyst feed enter through the input port, pass first through reaction unit 1 for the initial catalytic incubation, and then go to the particle filter stage.
